{ignore}
<p><code>{page 'name'}</code> builds another page of the same application in a nested engine
pass and puts its output where the tag stands. It is not a second request: the page's PHP runs
in this request, its template renders, and the result is the tag's value. The page is named
from the application root, as in a URL, and resolved as the router resolves one - a directory
means its <code>index</code>, a <code>.html</code> template counts, and a clean URL route
binds its segments, so <code>{page 'products/42'}</code> renders <code>products/[id].pad</code>
with <code>$id</code> set to 42.</p>

<p>By default the page renders bare (<code>include</code> is TRUE): the
<code>_inits.pad</code> and <code>_exits.pad</code> wrappers are not added a second time.
The page's directory guards still decide - a <code>_guard.php</code> that refuses makes the
tag render as nothing instead of refusing the whole request. A page that does not exist is an
error under the strict check; without it the tag is empty and the rest of the page lives.</p>

<h3>How isolated the page is</h3>

<p>A plain <code>{page}</code> shares the request: it reads the variables set before it, and
what its PHP creates stays behind for the rest of the page. Four options change that, the same
four <a href="?tag/code">{code}</a> takes:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>sandbox</code> - the page sees nothing of the page around it and leaves nothing
  behind (the <a href="?tag/sandbox">{sandbox}</a> tag runs content this way);</li>
  <li><code>reset</code> - the levels and stores are cleared for the pass, the variables
  stay;</li>
  <li><code>clean</code> - the page sees everything, but what it changes is undone
  afterwards;</li>
  <li><code>function='name'</code> - the page's PHP runs inside a PHP function, with a scope
  of its own.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Other applications</h3>

<p>With <code>app='other'</code> the page belongs to another application, with its own
configuration, <code>_lib</code> and database, so it cannot be built here: it is fetched over
HTTP. By default the browser fetches it, as <a href="?tag/ajax">{ajax}</a> does;
<code>ajax=false</code> has the server fetch it and insert its body. For a page of this
application fetched as a real second request, see <a href="?tag/get">{get}</a>.</p>
{/ignore}
